numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4x}{2{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{4x}{x(2{x}^{2}+5x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4x}{x(2x+1)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cancel{x}}{\\cancel{x}(2x+1)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(2x+1)(x+2)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n(2x+1)(x+2)=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions  $x=-2$ and $x=-\\,\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-2$ and $x=-\\,\\frac{1}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523962 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523962 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523962 #edb/ref 1069 "q-278259" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523962 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523962 #edb/ref 1071 "In **ascending order**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+4x+3}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523962 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6baac323-becf-503f-bb38-28a603f7f3c3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523962 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{2}{(x+3)(x+1)}.\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n(x+3)(x+1)=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=-3$ and $x=-1.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-3$ and $x=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523981 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523981 #edb/ref 1069 "q-80681" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523981 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523981 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{5x}{{x}^{2}-x+2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523981 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "86d3c939-36b2-5329-b38e-94ddc558ef8b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523981 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nNote that the equation ${x}^{2}-x+2=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}-4(1)(2) \\\\  & =1-8 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523997 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186523809 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523997 #edb/ref 1069 "q-70552" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523997 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523997 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523997 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a5878b34-7fd0-5b9f-89d8-91c764ed89b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186523997 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x-1}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-1}{(x+1)(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x-1}}{(x+1)\\cancel{(x-1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx+1=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=-1.$ Therefore, $x=-1$ is the only vertical asymptote." #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
